Instead of using the remainder operator, this version repeatedly subtracts the smaller value from the larger one. The common divisor does not change when this subtraction is made.

The loop stops when both values become equal. A zero check is needed first, because subtracting with zero would never make progress. This method is readable, but it takes more steps than the remainder-based Euclidean method when the numbers are far apart.

```java
class Solution {
    public int GCD(int n1, int n2) {
        n1 = n1 < 0 ? -n1 : n1;
        n2 = n2 < 0 ? -n2 : n2;

        if (n1 == 0) {
            return n2;
        }
        if (n2 == 0) {
            return n1;
        }

        while (n1 != n2) {
            if (n1 > n2) {
                n1 -= n2;
            } else {
                n2 -= n1;
            }
        }

        return n1;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(max(n1, n2))` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Edge cases covered here include equal numbers, one zero input, and negative inputs.

A recursive version is short because each call only needs the current pair of numbers. When the second number reaches zero, the first number is the answer.

The absolute value checks are done before the recursion. This keeps the result non-negative and also handles cases where one input is zero. The recursion uses stack space, unlike the iterative version.

```java
class Solution {
    public int GCD(int n1, int n2) {
        n1 = n1 < 0 ? -n1 : n1;
        n2 = n2 < 0 ? -n2 : n2;
        return findGcd(n1, n2);
    }

    private int findGcd(int first, int second) {
        if (second == 0) {
            return first;
        }
        return findGcd(second, first % second);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log(min(n1, n2)))` for positive inputs.
Space complexity: `O(log(min(n1, n2)))` because of the recursive calls.

The binary method uses powers of two instead of division. If both numbers are even, their common factor includes `2`. If only one is even, divide that number by two. Once both are odd, subtract the smaller from the larger and continue.

Dry run with `48` and `18` starts by removing one factor of two from both values, leaving `24` and `9`. The remaining odd and even reductions eventually leave `3`, and the saved factor of two gives `6`.

This version is a different way to reach the same result. It is a little less familiar than the normal remainder method, but it avoids remainder operations.

```python
class Solution:
    def GCD(self, n1, n2):
        a = abs(n1)
        b = abs(n2)

        if a == 0:
            return b
        if b == 0:
            return a

        common_power = 0
        while ((a | b) & 1) == 0:
            a >>= 1
            b >>= 1
            common_power += 1

        while (a & 1) == 0:
            a >>= 1

        while b != 0:
            while (b & 1) == 0:
                b >>= 1

            if a > b:
                a, b = b, a

            b -= a

        return a << common_power
```

Time complexity: `O(log(max(n1, n2))^2)` with this simple subtraction form of the binary method.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The zero cases are handled before any bit shifting, so the method also works when one input is zero.

The remainder gets smaller after every step, so the problem becomes much simpler than checking every possible divisor. Keep replacing `(n1, n2)` with `(n2, n1 % n2)` until the second value becomes zero.

Dry run for `48` and `18`:
- `48 % 18 = 12`
- `18 % 12 = 6`
- `12 % 6 = 0`

The answer is `6`. The code also uses absolute values, so negative inputs do not change the result. If both numbers are zero, it returns zero.

