## Intuition
Used Mathematical GCD Euclidean Algorithm: GCD(a,b) = GCD(b, a % b)

## Approach
I'm using the Euclidean Algorithm. The idea is that the GCD of two numbers remains the same if we replace the larger number with its remainder after dividing by the smaller number. So in each iteration, I transform (n1, n2) into (n2, n1 % n2). The numbers keep getting smaller until the remainder becomes zero. At that point, the current value of n1 is the GCD. This approach is much more efficient than checking every divisor because it runs in O(log(min(n1, n2))) time and uses O(1) space.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int GCD(int n1, int n2) {
        while (n2 != 0) {
            int temp = n2;
            n2 = n1 % n2;
            n1 = temp;
        }
        return Math.abs(n1);
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(log(min(n1, n2))) due to the Euclidean algorithm which reduces the numbers logarithmically per iteration

**Space Complexity:**
O(1) because it only uses a constant amount of extra space for the integer variables temp, n1, and n2

